# 5. The Plan for Mainnet #

# Everything so far runs on a local replica. On mainnet: #

# CYCLES. The canister pays for every update in cycles: each remote #
# run and each write. A demo-sized top-up is enough to start; the #
# canister should report its balance, and refuse remote runs below a #
# floor rather than run dry. #

# LIMITS. One update call has an instruction budget. A long Fumola #
# run must be chunked: the runtime already has eval_top_limited and #
# resume, which the page uses to show progress, so the canister can #
# answer paused and the page ask again. #

# CERTIFIED READS. Reads now go through update calls, which are #
# slow but trustworthy. Fast query reads need certified responses, #
# a hash of the state the replica signs. #

# ACCESS. Open writes are for the demo. After it: writes to a space #
# by the principals it lists, reads open or listed per space, and a #
# rate limit on remote runs. #

# UPGRADES. Spaces survive an upgrade, through a versioned snapshot. #
# Remote instances do not: a Fumola state is not serializable, and a #
# page rebuilds its instance by running its program again. #
()
